description:
Lesson 2.01 compared `std::stoi` and `std::from_chars`. Use their reference pages to implement two functions with very different contracts.

```cpp-norun
std::optional<int> parseStrict(std::string_view text);

struct Leading
{
    std::optional<int> value;
    std::size_t used;
    std::string error;
};
Leading readLeadingInt(const std::string& text);
```

**parseStrict** accepts exactly: an optional single `+` or `-`, then one or more decimal digits, and **nothing else**: no spaces anywhere, no other characters, nothing after the digits. The value must fit in an `int`. Anything else gives an empty optional. (Note that `std::from_chars` itself does not accept a leading `+`.)

**readLeadingInt** behaves like `std::stoi` in base 10, and reports what happened instead of throwing:

- Leading whitespace is skipped, then an optional sign and as many digits as follow are read; reading stops at the first other character.
- On success: `value` holds the number, `used` the count of characters processed (whitespace included), `error` is empty.
- If there is no number at the start: `value` empty, `used` 0, `error` is `"no number"`.
- If the number does not fit in an `int`: `value` empty, `used` 0, `error` is `"out of range"`.

